Adventure Creator v1.12a (c) 1995-1999 Chris Jones

The Adventure Creator (AC for short) allows you to create Sierra-style
adventure games. It incorporates the point 'n' click interface and has
walk, look, interact and speak (or man, eye, hand and head). At present
it does not include smell or taste, or any other more unusual icons.
Normally it would take a company weeks or months to make a game, but
you can do it in a matter of hours (or days depending on your artistic skill!)
And you don't need any programming experience to use the Adventure Creator:
all you need is a little knowledge of DOS (so I don't have to tell you how to
change directory, etc). And for those of you who are more experienced, the
Adventure Creator supports scripting: a means by which you can write a sort of
program, making the Creator even more powerful.
The Adventure Creator handles many tasks for you, like load/save game, keeping
track of inventory, and the player interface.

Why not print this file and use it as a tutorial? Just type PRINT AC.DOC at
the DOS prompt. WARNING: This requires about 20 sheets of paper.

This file consists of six sections:
 1. Using the run-time engine
 2. Creating your own games: Tutorial
 3. Other features
 4. Command reference
 5. Troubleshooting
 6. Miscellaneous/contact info
 7. Technical Info
 8. Disclaimer

This document is set up with a quick search capability, to enable you to go
straight to any part of the text - press F7 now (assuming you're looking at
this in VIEW).
The first section explains the interface to the actual game engine.
Work through (or just read) the tutorial next, which will teach you how to do
the basic things involved in creating games. The Other Features section
describes more advanced features not explained in the tutorial. Refer to the
command reference for a complete list of information on various topics.

The Adventure Creator has these system requirements:
  - A 386 or above processor (486/66 recommended, P133 recommended for SVGA).
  - A Microsoft-compatible mouse with driver loaded.
  - VGA (or better) 256-colour card and monitor. SVGA req'd for Room Editor.
  - 4 Mb RAM
  - Optional Adlib and Sound Blaster support.

