6.4 CONTACTING THE AUTHOR
If you have any comments/suggestions, mail me at  dosuser@hotmail.com
If you think you've found a bug, if you need help or can't get it to work,
please check the following:
 * That your question is not already answered in this file. The Creator has
   been painstakingly documented, so PLEASE read this file through before
   asking me a question.
 * That you are using the most recent version of the Creator. You can
   find out the most recent version by checking the website (address below).
   You can find out your version by typing  AC /?  and noting the line that
   reads "Adventure Creator vx.xx" (x.xx = version number), and the line which
   reads "Revision DD.MM.YY.R" (date/month/year/sub-version).
 * If you are receiving an error message, please write down the EXACT message
   and any other information which you think is appropriate. PLEASE WRITE THE
   EXACT ERROR MESSAGE TEXT, don't just say "I get a message about it not being
   able to find a file".
 * If you are getting lock-ups or techno-garbage printouts, please try to
   give a situation which causes the problem. For instance, if you notice that
   it only happens on certain screens, or only if there is an object, etc then
   please state that - it makes replying a lot easier and faster.
Please note: I usually check my e-mail two or three times a week, so give me
about a week to reply before assuming your message didn't get through.
The AC website:  http://members.xoom.com/dosuser/ac.htm

6.5 TIPS FOR DESIGNING GAMES
Here are a few points which seem to make a better game:
 - Try not to make it too linear. If the player has to go through room 1,
   then room 2, and so on, it can get boring and the player will easily give
   up if they get stuck.
   The best games, such as Quest for Glory by Sierra, have one main game
   area, made up of several screens, in which the whole game takes place,
   with the occasional extra screen for when the player completes part of
   the quest. You'll notice that most of Sierra's games were made with this
   in mind. For example, all the King's Quest series use the same idea, as
   does Space Quest III (remember how you could go between the three planets).
 - Put in alternative ways of acheiving certain goals. For example, to get
   past the dragon in King's Quest I you can either throw water at it OR use
   your invisibility ring. You can then give the player more points for the
   harder option.
 - Try to put in optional extra quests, like the Quest for Glory series has,
   which don't have to be done to complete the game but will give the player
   more points if they do them. This can mean that the player will come back
   and play a second time - the problem with adventure games is that if they
   score maximum points the first time they complete it, chances are they
   will never play it again.
 - Try to get the proper 3D perspective when drawing the background scenes.
   The whole game can look strange otherwise. You'll notice this in the demo
   game, how all the lines on the pavements slope left (which is wrong - take
   a look at the corridors in Space Quest I to see how it should be done).

